Expose a real, trusted command-execution bridge for the native iOS host
app to call, instead of it having to simulate keybindings.

ipad-vscode's native iPadOS-26 menu bar (Save/Undo/Redo/Find/Command
Palette/etc. -- see ipad-vscode/Sources/App/iPadVSCodeApp.swift) needs a
way to actually trigger these vscode commands from outside the webview
when a menu item is clicked with no physical key involved. The only
mechanism tried first (see ipad-vscode git history) was dispatching a
synthetic KeyboardEvent that mimics the real keybinding -- but vscode's
own keybinding service listens for real `keydown` DOM events, and
whether it filters on `event.isTrusted` (false for anything
JS-dispatched) was an open, unverified question with no clean way to
answer short of shipping it and finding out. Even setting that aside, it
has a second real problem: simulating a *keybinding* only works for
whatever key the command happens to be bound to at that moment -- if the
user remaps it in their own keybindings.json, the native menu item
would silently send the wrong (now-stale) keystroke.

This patch sidesteps both problems by not simulating input at all.
`Workbench.startup()` (workbench.ts) already resolves `ICommandService`
via its `instantiationService.invokeFunction(accessor => ...)` block --
this adds `accessor.get(ICommandService)` there too and exposes
`(mainWindow as any).__ipadVSCodeExecuteCommand = (commandId, ...args) =>
commandService.executeCommand(commandId, ...args)`. The native side then
calls `window.__ipadVSCodeExecuteCommand('workbench.action.files.save')`
via WKWebView's `evaluateJavaScript` -- itself a trusted native-to-JS
call, nothing to do with the DOM input-event trust model at all -- which
is a completely ordinary function call through the same ICommandService
every other trusted caller inside the workbench already uses. Invoking
by command ID also means it keeps working correctly regardless of
whatever key (if any) the user has that command bound to, since it never
goes through the keybinding-to-command lookup in the first place.

Verified the same way ios-exthost-no-fork.diff was: the intended edits
were applied to a real copy of the pinned vscode commit's source
(08d4889f9ec4a1685d257b9b95de036c8e1ce1e5) and diffed programmatically
rather than hand-writing hunk headers, then confirmed to apply with
`patch -p1 --fuzz=0` (zero tolerance for near-miss context) with the
resulting file byte-identical to the intended target. Not run through
`tsc` for real (no local vscode build environment in this pipeline).

